< +const AO_ABOUT = 'Turning plane, along squares every point about a plane of its own, at every step. That gives c = −1 its buds in eight directions, and it swells the rabbit into one lump. This entry does it once. The first squaring of an orbit is the one of Turning plane, along, and from the second squaring on the orbit follows Sphere inversions, one plane. So a point belongs to a Julia set of this entry exactly when its first step lands in the solid that Sphere inversions, one plane draws for the same c. What was measured in round 21, point by point, and seen in cuts: for c = −1 the middle body has eight buds round it (eight separate stretches of the set on circles round the x-axis through the buds). Close up the eight are not alike. The two in the plane y = 0 carry smaller buds on their sides, as in Turning plane, along. The two in the flat plane are bare on their sides, as in Sphere inversions, one plane. The four at 45° are plain lenses. The rabbit comes apart into smooth bodies, also off the flat plane, where Turning plane, along has one lump. Where it falls short: the bodies to the left and right of the middle body of c = −1 are bare off the flat plane. Cut at 45° and at 90° they show no buds. For c = −0.75 the bodies are fused into one mass. The rabbit is in three pieces: two of its bodies float free, one above and one below the rest. Each holds 7.2% of the volume and stands 0.11 away from the rest, the same on a coarse grid and on a fine one, so the gap is real. For a c out of the plane z = 0 it does no better than Sphere inversions, one plane: with the rabbit’s c turned 10° or more about the x-axis no point of the set was left. The Mandelbrot solid has the body of the solid of Sphere inversions, one plane: of 200,000 points c tested with 80 steps in round 21, none got a different answer. But the picture also shows thin curved wings standing off its left end, where Sphere inversions, one plane has nothing: in one picture 68 of 195 rays stopped on them. The wings are there, but they are films with no thickness. Followed along a line of sight into three of them, the orbit stays longer and longer the nearer the film (18 to 22 steps at the best of 20,000 places along a stretch 0.08 long, 39 to 57 steps when the search is narrowed to a stretch of 0.000000000003), and no place was found that stays for good. And it is not one rule: it is two rules and a count that says when to change from one to the other, which nothing in the flat rule asks for. Powers other than 2 have not been looked at for this entry.';
