< +const PP_ABOUT = 'Sphere inversions, one plane squares every point about the flat page. Turning plane, along gives every point a plane of its own. Here the plane belongs to the orbit. Think of the planes through the x-axis as the pages of a book whose spine is the x-axis. Every orbit starts with the book open at the flat page, held weakly. Before each squaring two things pull at the page: first c, then the point that is about to be squared. A thing far from the x-axis pulls hard, and a thing on the x-axis does not pull at all. Each pulls toward the page it lies in itself, less directly the nearer that page is to the open one. The pulls add up and none is forgotten, so the page turns less and less as the orbit goes on. Then the point is squared by the one-plane rule about the page as it stands. What was measured in round 21, point by point, and seen in cuts: for c = −1 a row of separate bodies with round buds in four directions, two in the flat plane and two in the plane y = 0 (four separate stretches of the set on circles round the x-axis through the buds, 19° and 15° wide, where Turning plane, along has eight of 13°). The cut along the plane y = 0 looks like the flat picture. The rabbit and the Siegel disc are clean separate bodies in one piece, where Turning plane, along has a lump. Where it falls short: the four directions are there for c on the x-axis and close to it only. For c = −1 + 0.2i nothing is left in the plane y = 0, and for a c a little off the x-axis a ledge runs across the front of every body. The rabbit is nearly the rabbit of Sphere inversions, one plane (one point in eight of its inside differs), so its smaller bodies sit round the flat page only. Close up, thin loose flakes hover over the skin of c = −1 between the two rows of buds: in 336 of 720 directions tested there, and still there after 600 steps, where Sphere inversions, one plane has none. At 45° there is no bud, only a thin loose crescent. The Siegel disc loses 15% of its points between 30 and 200 steps before it settles. For a c that lies in neither of the two planes the set can vanish: with the rabbit’s c turned by 10°, 30° or 45° about the x-axis no point of it was left. The Mandelbrot solid shows the flat set in the plane z = 0 and, but for a few points, in the plane y = 0 (104 of 84,000 places differ), with smooth skin between the two and no buds there. Three numbers of the rule were set by hand, and they matter: see the three dials. Powers other than 2 have not been looked at for this entry.';
